  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 155 AM CDT Sun Sep 6 2026\n\nA few minor concerns early this morning. The first, satellite\nimagery was showing patches of low clouds/marine fog just east of\nTwo Rivers, and well east of Manitowoc towards mid lake. The area\nwas drifting westward earlier but has shown little movement of \nlate. The second concern is KDLH radar was showing some radar \nechoes into Vilas County. Couldn't rule out a few sprinkles over \nthe next few hours across north-central WI. \n\nFor today and Labor Day, skies will be partly cloudy with highs\naround 80 as the stretch of above normal temperatures continue.\nThe normal high for this time of year is in the lower to middle\n70s, just a fact that the cooler days of fall are around the\ncorner. Attention then turns to approaching cold front Monday \nnight. It appear the chances of rain won't arrive until ate late \nacross north-central WI. \n\nThe Tuesday into Wednesday time frame is the period of main \nconcern. For marine interest, hazardous conditions for small craft\ndevelop on Tuesday due to the gusty southerly winds and building \nwaves which will continue into Tuesday night. Winds will veer to \nthe west on Wednesday, but will still be gusty to create hazardous\nconditions for small craft. If you are heading to Lake Michigan \nbeaches, there will be an increased risk of rip currents \nTuesday into Wednesday morning. \n\nAs the cold front moves across the region Tuesday and Tuesday\nnight, thunderstorms are expected to develop along and ahead of \nit. Looking at some of the convective parameters, enough shear and\ninstability available for stronger storms. The Storm Prediction \nCenter Day 3 Outlook has a portion of our area, our western\ncounties, in a Marginal Risk of severe thunderstorms. \n\nThursday and Friday are expected to be dry with the next chance\nof showers/storms on Friday night and Saturday. Above normal \ntemperatures to continue into next weekend.",
